Z||000004||Carrolles||04-03-2000||12:39 PM||||I remember a few years ago at Ed Park's Fiero Factory seeing a car that just got a 3.1L with the 4spd auto overdrive tranny out of a Cutlass Ciera.  Seemed like a pretty nice fit.||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||04-03-2000||05:06 PM||||If you are going to swap auto's do not get a 4T60E unless you plan on swapping computers. The 4T60 (also known as the THM440) is the only real choice. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||04-03-2000||06:29 PM||||the 440 is about the same as the 125 as far as I can tell. It still uses a TV cable but i believe it also adds a vacuum modulator. Both have to be adjusted correctly or you'll have shifting problems.<P>It's a bolt in as far as the bell housing goes. Not sure about mounts. I think you may need the 440 upper brackets, but the isolators should be ok. <P>I'm not 100% sure but I think the axles are the same for the 440. You could verify this easy enough by finding something that was available with both the 125 and the 440 and checking its axle listing.<P>I'd like to know myself. I may find out soon. My 125 is ok but old. What I do know for sure is that many GM TSB's for the 125 also apply to the 440. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||olympic||04-03-2000||11:30 PM||||   The 4T60E is useable.  You just need a stand alone computer controller for the tranny.  I know they make something like this for the North Star tranny for use with a chevy V8.   ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Dan86gt3.8||04-04-2000||03:24 AM||||ya I have the 4t60e in mine.  and yes the ecm does run it there is a 4  or 6 wire plug that goes to it from the wiring harness.  for the axles you have to use the ineers of the 4t60e and the outers of a fiero to fit into the splines in the spindles, also check for clearence on the passenger side, cause if you don't you will have chronic problems with the boot rubbing the frame and sliding off.  Take it from me I have first hand experience!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000009||terryk||04-04-2000||11:24 PM||||4T60E is longer on the passenger side so a shorter axle is needed.<P>The 440 is basically a 125C with overdrive added. The 4T60 is a 440 with some of the upgrades. Get that before a 440.<P>I've been pretty brutal to my 3800SC/4T60E and at 120K, so far so good. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||04-05-2000||10:13 AM||||I'm guessing that means that both the mounting brackets are the same and the ECM connector for TC locking are the same?<P>Are you using the same shifter hardware as the 125 or did you have to make something up?<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||mrfixit58||04-05-2000||10:34 AM||||I like the 4T60E idea. Does anyone know in what years and model of cars that these transmissions started showing?<P>terryk - You  mentioned the passenger side is shorter, does this mean that a stock (4T60E?) axle can be used on the drivers side? And, I assume, the passenger side axle just needs to be shortened and resplined?<P>Roy<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||terryk||04-06-2000||11:58 AM||||I have to do some logic here. My 4T60E/3800SC is in my Ciera GT.<P>The Ciera comes with the 125C or 440/4T60 which use the same axle lengths although the 440/4T60 use larger axles and hubs (100mm vs 115mm bolt pattern)<P>Now the 4T60E uses the same diameter axles and hubs as the 440/4T60, but the passenger side axle is short (the 4T60E is a bit longer.<P>Ergo, in a Fiero, which comes with the 125C, I would expect to need the shorter passenger 4T60E axle shaft and the driver's side would not change. <P>CV's Unlimited is a great place to call about the available axle lengths. They will combine CV's, axles and splines if needed. The guy there could rattle off axle lengths off the top of his head. ||||reg||1
Z||000013||mrfixit58||04-06-2000||02:46 PM||||Wow,<P>You guys never cease to amaze me. Not ony with the machanical stuff but all that wizz-bang computer and sensor stuff as well.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>Now, does anyone know which years the 4T60 started show up???  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Roy||||reg||14
Z||000014||GREENDANO||04-08-2000||01:51 PM||||4T60E's started showing up around 1991.  If you're getting an older 4T60, get one from the later 80's.  The early ones had continuous failures, similar to the 4L60 (700R4)  The public was GM's test bed and they updated and improved them each year as a problem arose.<BR>  Dan-O||||reg||1
Z||000015||Raydar||04-10-2000||12:38 PM||||Okay, please allow me to further confuse things. I understood that a 440-T4 was the same as a 125C with the addition of an overdrive 4th gear. Came in Cavaliers and Sunbirds, and some of the other small cars, usually behind a 2.8. I also understood that a 4L60 was a completely different, larger transmission. Came in Bonnevilles and Grand Prixs. Usually behind a 3.8.<BR>This is based upon some things that I've read, and some creative "filling-in-the-blanks." <BR>I don't know if this is correct. Anyone?||||reg||1
Z||000016||terryk||04-10-2000||03:04 PM||||T means transverse and L means longitudinal, so that limits where the trans where used. T's go in FWD and L's go in RWD's.<P>The THM440 and it's later version 4T60 were used behind 2.8's, 3.1, 3.3, 3.8. 4.1, 4.5.<P>The 4T60E's were used on 90 and up 3.8's. 4T60EHD's (heavy duty) were used on 91 and up 3.4L DOHC's and 3800SC's. The 4T65E which is a different looking 4T60E but more similar than different internally was added in 97 for the 3800SC engines. (The specs from Powertrain are the same. The 4T65E can handle the larger torque converter and has shift quality control switches). <P>The 4T80E was added in 92-ish for the Northstar 4.6 and later Aurora 4.0's. Not sure of the year.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Old Lar||04-10-2000||10:34 PM||||Twin Lakes Fiero is putting in a 440 in a 3.4L conversion for me.  I wanted the higher gear (4th) for highway cruising.  The T125 ran at 3200 rpm at 70 and sucked gas.  I'm hoping it all works out.||||reg||3
Z||000018||mrfixit58||04-11-2000||10:07 AM||||EXCELLENT!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Lar,<P>Please see if you can confirm that the 440 uses the same axles as the 125. If no, are they using a stock axle and, if so, which model.<P>How are they adapting the shifter? Is the speedo a plug-and-play?<P>Sorry for all the questions but, if you can get these answered, I'm sure I'll be using it for a future replacement. It would also make a great "how to" article.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/cool.gif"><P>Roy||||reg||14
